7. GOLDPRISM X-RAY WATERFALL
==================================================

This is the signature feature and must be implemented as a real analytical workflow, not merely a decorative chart.

The X-Ray engine should explain the observed spread between comparable gold contracts by decomposing it into defensible components.

Potential components:
- Normalized raw spread.
- Economic basis differences.
- Expiry and carry effects.
- Settlement or data artifacts.
- Estimated residual.
- Transaction costs.
- Net residual or net opportunity estimate.

Do not assume that every component is directly observable. Document the estimation method, assumptions, limitations, and uncertainty.

Avoid double-counting components.

Use mathematically consistent calculations with reconciliation checks so that the components sum to the defined raw spread within a documented tolerance.

Display:
- Raw spread.
- Each estimated component.
- Residual.
- Estimated transaction costs.
- Net estimated edge.
- Confidence or uncertainty information.
- Data-quality status.
- Explanation of each component.
- Reasons the signal may be unreliable.

Use a waterfall chart and supporting detail panels.

If there is insufficient data to produce a valid decomposition, show why the analysis is unavailable rather than inventing values.

